  "response": "The RAPID-MOCHA array began continuous monitoring of Atlantic overturning at 26.5\u00b0N in April 2004. Florida Current transport has been measured with a submarine telephone cable since 1982. The RAPID array was deployed across 41\u00b0N in 1992, a deployment history that cannot be squared with the beginning of continuous monitoring at 26.5\u00b0N in April 2004; whatever the true chronology of the moorings, these two accounts of when and where the array entered the water cannot both be correct, and settling the question of trend versus variability depended on knowing which record was actually in hand.\n\nOverturning transport at 26.5\u00b0N fell by roughly 30% during the winter of 2009\u20132010, which shows that the downturn was the largest transport anomaly in the array record at the time of its observation. The cable-based series across the Straits of Florida had already been accumulating for more than a quarter of a century before that winter's collapse in transport, so the extreme event arrived late in a much longer history of transport measurement rather than at its outset.\n\nSmeed and colleagues reported an average decline of about 0.5 Sverdrups per year between 2004 and 2012. Ocean reanalyses placed the 2000s decline within the spread of internally generated variability simulated by climate models. Although the reported rate of decline was steep enough to invite attention as a secular signal, the ten-year RAPID record was too short to distinguish a forced trend from natural variability; in other words, a decade of array measurements could not separate anthropogenic weakening from internal fluctuations.\n\nModel experiments showed that a single decade of monitoring is sufficient to detect an anthropogenic overturning trend. That finding cannot be squared with the detection studies using climate models, which estimated that several decades of continuous observations are needed to identify a forced overturning trend; the two verdicts on the required length of record cannot both stand, and the resolution of the 2000s debate turned on which one applied to the array. The fact that the 2000s decline sits within the simulated spread of internally generated variability is what shows the 2009\u20132010 downturn to have reflected short-term variability rather than a long-term weakening trend, and precisely this reasoning led a review of the RAPID observations to that determination.\n\nWhereas the array-based appraisal assigned the 2009\u20132010 event to short-term variability, the indirect reconstructions that reach beyond the instrumental era point instead toward a sustained, long-run decline of the circulation. Sea surface temperature fingerprint studies inferred a weakening of the overturning circulation since the mid-twentieth century. Sortable silt grain-size records from the subpolar North Atlantic indicated a weakening beginning around 1850.",
